{
  "requirement": "`kind` classifies the construct and `span` locates it under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span`. Language providers can\nconstruct an attachment from these two fields.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OutlineAttachment {\n    pub kind: AttachmentKind,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\nimpl OutlineAttachment {\n    pub fn new(kind: AttachmentKind, span: Span) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OutlineAttachment;\nimpl Debug for OutlineAttachment;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no test-adequacy gap for `requirements.sdk.outlines.OutlineAttachment`.\n\nObligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n- **Constructor and fields.**\n  - `new(kind, span)` must build an attachment from the two fields, and `kind` and `span` must be public.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` builds attachments only through `OutlineAttachment::new(AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\".into()), *span)`. I read that in the nested `impl[Custom/OutlineLang]/fn[outline]`.\n  - After loading, the test reads `attachment.kind` and `attachment.span` as public fields. It asserts `kind == AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\")` and `&snapshot.text()[attachment.span] == \"é\"`. A constructor that swapped or dropped either value would fail these assertions.\n  - `fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` also calls `new` with `AttachmentKind::Comment` and `AttachmentKind::Annotation`. It does not reference the subject, so I treated it as extra evidence only.\n- **Span located under the cited `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span`.** This is a cited requirement, so it is context here, not something to reassess.\n  - The custom-language test applies it to the subject. A span slices the original snapshot at UTF-8 byte boundaries and yields the whole construct.\n  - Spans that split a character (`Span::new(1,2)`) or fall outside the snapshot are rejected.\n  - Spans that extend past the item or start before it (`Span::new(0,3)`) are rejected.\n  - Adjacent and out-of-order spans are checked as well.\n- **Shape, `#[non_exhaustive]`, and `Clone`/`Debug` implementations.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` generates rustdoc for the SDK. Through `check_declaration`, it compares the documented declaration listing, member names and signatures, and the trait-implementation set to rustdoc. It asserts `expected_impls == rustdoc.trait_impls(page)`, so a missing `Clone` or `Debug` impl, a changed field, or a changed `new` signature would fail. `Clone` and `Debug` have no conditional logic of their own, so a separate behavioral test adds little.\n\nI found no acknowledgments, exemptions, or ambiguities that affect this requirement.",
